 there was surprisingly little movement  from either side uh volumizing making no  secret of his disappointment there  basically saying that the promises and  the compensation offered by germany in  return for this pipeline being completed  uh the attempt to sweeten the pill as it  were was too little too late and not  really tangible he said that that loss  of income for transit fees but the  bigger strategic issue that you know  russia could afford to destabilize  ukraine if it doesn't need ukraine's  pipelines to transport its gas to europe  that that was an immediate tangible cost  in the short term and that investments  in green energy or potential sanctions  should russia once again  be aggressive towards ukraine that was  all potentially unenforceable so really  um a pretty  pretty pretty gray pretty kind of chilly  response there and no sense that  ukrainian side was really  convinced by the arguments brought by  angela merkel and her team well we  mentioned nick that angela merkel is on  something of a farewell tour and she got  a warm public welcome from vladimir  putin in moscow on friday how did that  compare to the reception that she got in  kiev  i think this is all the question of  expectations uh in the case of russia  given what's happened over the last 10  years uh from crimea to donbass and now  the poisoning alexander navalny last  year i think the very fact that they're  still talking is deemed to be a success  here in kiev the expectations are much  higher as you send your introduction  ukraine and germany are essentially  allies i mean germany is one of the  biggest donors to ukraine angela merkel  invested lots of diplomatic capital and  time back in 2014-15 bringing those  negotiations uh into fruition making  sure that this country was stabilized so  the fact that it has ended on such a  chilly note that she didn't get a kind  of cozy farewell trip to kiev is all the  more surprising um but i think it's also  a question of tone i think the  ukrainians want more forthright support  less diplomatic caution uh more  willingness in berlin to call out russia  and maybe just essentially give  ukrainians a sense that they have their  back and that they are not somehow  balancing on the fence trying to kind of  uh play the intermediary between moscow  and and kiev so a disappointment there  but recognition still that angela merkel  is someone who always found time for  ukraine  dw's nick connolly reporting for us in  kiev good to talk to you  you
 

